{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a service offered by AWS, has expanded its support for Batch inference for two new models: Anthropic's Claude Sonnet 4 and OpenAI's GPT-OSS 120B and 20B. With Batch inference, users can process multiple inference requests concurrently, improving performance and reducing costs by up to 50% compared to on-demand inference pricing","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/amazon-bedrock-batch-inference-anthropic-claude-sonnet-4-openai-gpt-oss-models/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Bedrock introduces Priority and Flex inference service tiers - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a service offered by AWS, has introduced two new inference service tiers: Flex and Priority. The Flex tier is designed for non-interactive workloads that can tolerate longer latencies, making it suitable for model evaluations, content summarization, labeling and annotation, and multistep agentic workflow. It is priced at a discount relative to the Standard tier but receives lower priority during periods of high demand. The Priority tier, on the other hand, is ideal for mission-critical applications, real-time end-user interactions, and interactive experiences where consistent, fast responses are essential. During periods of high demand, Priority requests receive processing priority over other service tiers at a premium price. These new service tiers are available for leading foundation models like OpenAI (gpt-oss-20b, gpt-oss-120b), DeepSeek (DeepSeek V3.1), Qwen3 (Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct, Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct, 32B dense, Qwen3-235B-A22B-2507), and Amazon Nova (Nova Pro and Nova Premier). These new options help customers balance cost efficiency with performance requirements and scale AI workloads economically while ensuring optimal user experiences for their most critical applications. The service tiers are available in various AWS regions. For more information, refer to the AWS Regions table and the service tiers' News Blog and documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-bedrock-priority-flex-inference-service-tiers/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"caching-for-llm-inference","skill_description":"Sets up a shared L2 KV cache for self-hosted LLM inference on Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey, so GPU servers load prefill KV state over EFA RDMA instead of recomputing it. Checks fit, estimates savings with KVTrace, builds a business case, creates the cache, connects vLLM or SGLang (also as NVIDIA Dynamo or llm-d workers) through an LMCache server, tunes for cost or latency, and troubleshoots. Use when a builder serves open-weight models on GPUs (for example H100 or B200, on Amazon EC2 or Amazon EKS) for multi-turn chat, agents, RAG, or long contexts and asks about KV cache offloading, prefix cache sharing, prompt caching for self-hosted models, LMCache with Valkey, or lower TTFT or GPU cost. Not for Bedrock prompt caching, LLM response or semantic caching, or general application caching.\n\nServices: elasticache, ec2\nTasks: deploy, optimize, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, data-scientist, devops\nWorkload: ai-ml","skill_name":"caching-for-llm-inference"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-billing-and-cost-management","skill_description":"Analyze AWS costs, find savings, manage budgets, evaluate Savings Plans and Reserved Instances, right-size EC2/Lambda/RDS/EBS with Compute Optimizer, look up service pricing, query CUR with Athena, detect cost anomalies, scope costs to billing views, and monitor Free Tier usage. Triggers on: AWS bill, cost analysis, reduce spend, savings plan, reserved instance, right-size, budget alert, cost optimization, pricing, free tier, cost anomaly, CUR, cost audit, billing view, billing view ARN.\n\n\nServices: cost-explorer, budgets, compute-optimizer, cost-optimization-hub, pricing, savings-plans, cur\nTasks: analyze-cost, optimize, budget, right-size, pricing-lookup, audit\nPersona: developer, finops-practitioner, cloud-engineer\nWorkload: cost-management\n*","skill_name":"aws-billing-and-cost-management"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify Bedrock batch inference pricing in docs"}

